            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Raich and Potter (1995) Q10 model",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The decomposition rate ds is computed by a Q10 model as described in the Appendix B. Biochemical mineralization is modeled using a rate constant Mmin if P limitation is absent (flimit = 1), but increases under P limitation until a maximum mineralization rate (Mmax) with a lag time of 30 days.\nThe respiration fluxes from litter-decomposing heterotrophs FlaA and FlwA are FiA = \u03b1i di Ci , (B1) where \u03b1i is the fraction of mineralized C which is respired to the atmosphere, and di the decomposition rate of Ci . The decomposition rates di of the non-lignified litter pool (i = la), the woody litter pool (i = lw), and the slow pool (i = s) are computed using a Q10 model (Raich and Potter, 1995): di = \u03be Q Tsoil/10\u25e6C 10 \u03c4i , (B2) where \u03be is soil humidity, and Tsoil is the temperature of the uppermost soil layer in \u25e6C. \u03c4i is the life time of pool i at 10 \u25e6C, and \u03be = 1 (Parida, 2010).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "JSBACH",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Langmuir equation",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "The equations governing the dynamics of labile and sorbed P (P1 and Psr) are adopted from Wang et al. (2010). It is assumed that the labile P pool and the sorbed P pool are in equilibrium on a daily time step. The relationship between the amount of labile P and sorbed P is described by the Langmuir equation (Barrow, 1978) in its differential form",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "JSBACH",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "empirical N fixation model",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "We use an empirical N fixation model which does not represent effects of changing P availability on N fixation, because the regulation mechanisms of N fixation are elusive and recent findings from a long term fertilization experiment reveal that N fixation in an intact tropical forest responded to molybdenum but not to P addition (Barron et al., 2009).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
